---
import Base from '../../layouts/Base.astro';
import Eca from '../../components/Eca.astro';
import EcaTable from '../../components/EcaTable.astro';
import Deck from '../../components/Deck.astro';
---
<Base title="Elementary cellular automata" description="An interactive elementary cellular automaton: any Wolfram rule 0–255, with a walkthrough of rule 90 for a crowd that plays it with their fingers." wide>
  <div class="measure">
    <h1>Elementary cellular automata</h1>
    <p>
      A row of cells, each either on or off. Time runs in ticks. At every tick each cell looks at three
      things only: its own state and the states of its left and right neighbour. A fixed rule says what
      it becomes next, and every cell switches at the same moment.
    </p>
    <p>
      Three cells make 8 possible neighbourhoods, and a rule assigns an outcome to each, so there are
      2<sup>8</sup> = 256 rules, numbered 0 to 255. In the picture below the top row is the starting
      state and every row underneath is the next tick: the image is a history, not a snapshot. The row
      is a ring, so the last cell counts the first as its right neighbour. Click the table under the
      controls to flip one outcome and see the rule number change.
    </p>
    <Deck id="deck" note="Fullscreen, two slides: the rule, then this demo. A clicker or the arrow keys move forward; Esc leaves.">
      <section data-slide aria-label="Rule 90" style="--lut-size: clamp(0.9rem, 1.9vw, 1.8rem)">
        <h2>Rule 90</h2>
        <EcaTable rule={90} fingers />
        <p>Look left, look right. Ignore your own hand.</p>
        <p>
          <strong>Exactly one neighbour up: your finger goes up.</strong><br />
          Both up, or both down: your finger goes down.
        </p>
        <p class="meta">Pick your two neighbours first and keep them. On the edge, take the person in front of you; if you cannot see two people, take the presenter.</p>
      </section>
      <section data-slide data-adopt="#eca" aria-label="Live demo"></section>
    </Deck>
  </div>

  <Eca rule={90} width={31} />

  <div class="measure">
    <p class="meta">
      Keys once the demo has focus (click it, go fullscreen or start presenting): space plays and
      pauses, → or PageDown steps, ← or PageUp undoes a round, r resets, f toggles fullscreen. A
      presentation clicker sends PageDown and PageUp. The address bar follows the settings, so a
      set-up can be bookmarked.
    </p>

    <h2 id="rule-90">Rule 90</h2>
    <EcaTable rule={90} fingers />
    <p>
      Read the eight outcomes from case 111 down to case 000 and you get the binary
      number <code>01011010</code>, which is 90 in decimal. That is where every rule gets its number.
    </p>
    <p>
      Now look at what the table actually says: the outcome is 1 exactly when the left and right
      neighbours differ. Your own state plays no part. In crowd terms: <strong>look left, look right,
      ignore your own hand. If exactly one of your two neighbours has a finger up, put yours up for
      the next round. If both or neither do, put it down.</strong> Mathematically the new state is
      left XOR right, the sum of the two neighbours modulo 2.
    </p>
    <p>
      Start from a single finger up in the middle and rule 90 draws the Sierpinski triangle: the
      pattern spreads one seat per round in both directions, and the inside keeps hollowing out into
      smaller copies of the whole. Row <em>t</em> is row <em>t</em> of Pascal's triangle with the odd
      entries marked, because adding modulo 2 is exactly what the rule does.
    </p>

    <h2 id="crowd">Running it with a seated crowd</h2>
    <ol>
      <li>Everyone picks two neighbours and keeps them for the whole game: normally the person on the
        left and the person on the right. On the edge of a row, take the person in front of you instead.
        If you cannot see two people, take the presenter.</li>
      <li>One person in the middle of the room starts with a finger up, everyone else down.</li>
      <li>Each round: look at your two neighbours, not at your own hand, and decide. Nobody moves yet. On
        "three", everyone changes at once. Deciding and changing have to be separate steps, or the quick
        ones will contaminate the round for the slow ones.</li>
      <li>Press <em>Start presenting</em> at the top of this page. The first slide is the rule as the
        crowd should hear it; one press forward on the clicker brings up the demo. From then on forward
        steps a round and back undoes one. Back at round 0 returns to the rule slide, and Esc leaves.
        Set the number of people as cells on the demo slide itself.</li>
      <li>The demo shows the clean case, one straight row of that many cells. A real room, with
        neighbours picked across rows, drifts away from the picture after a few rounds. The rule is the
        same; the wiring is not, and that is worth saying out loud.</li>
    </ol>
  </div>
</Base>
